<div class="paragraph">
    <h2>the structure</h2>
    <p>every lambda calculus term is built like a binary tree, with three types of nodes:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>
            <b>applications <code>(_ _)</code></b><br>they always have two kids, the left one we call its <b>function</b>, the right one its <b>argument</b>
        </li><li>
            <b>abstractions <code>λ_._</code></b><br> they also have two kids, the left one is its <b>bound variable</b>, and is always a variable, the right is its <b>body</b>
        </li><li>
            <b>variables <code>x</code></b><br> these are always a leaf, and have an identifier of some sort, usually a lowercase letter.
        </li>
    </ol>
    <p>usually lambda terms are written down using text, but i think its pretty unintuitive at first so i've written a nice visualizer for you, you can build up a little intuition for how terms look, and how they're written down i hope. also i made it link all the bound variables together cause it looks pretty.</p>
    <p>oh also, because the terms get really big usually, there's some shorthand you should know. <code>λx.λy.M</code> can be shortened to <code>λx y.M</code>, note the space between the variables. <code>((X Y) Z)</code>can be shortened to just <code>X Y Z</code> - application is <it>left-associative</it> - and ofcourse we omit the brackets if they're obvious. oh also you can use <code>\</code> instead of <code>λ</code></p>
    <p>play around with this a bit, then continue :3</p>
    <div class="lc-sim">
        <div class="buttons">
            <select class="examples">
                <option value="" selected disabled>pick a term to visualize</option>
