    </div>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
    <h2>beta reduction</h2>
    <p>you might've noticed that some of the applications visualized are filled with orange. you might've clicked them too, which made the term change suddenly. what you've just done is called <b>beta reduction</b> ! this represents applying function, basically the same as you're used to in math: if you have some function <code>f(x) := x^2 - x + 6</code>, and you evaluate it on input <code>3</code>, you get <code>f(3)</code> = <code>3^2 - 3 + 6</code>, all the x's got replaced with a copy of the argument.</p>
    <p>beta reduction works the exact same way. if you have an application, whose left kid is an abstraction, <code>(\x.B) A</code>, you can reduce it into <code>B[x/A]</code>, i.e. a version of B where all x's are replaced with A. its called <it>beta</it> reduction, because there is another one, called <it>alpha</it> reduction, that one is about renaming variables so that there's no duplicates. that also makes sense, you dont want a function like <code>f(x, x) = 2x - 3x</code>, you don't know which <code>x</code> is which.</p>
    <br>
    <p>look back at the example with c's and d's, and think about what both of the terms are doing. <code>λc.c c</code> takes in some argument, creates two copies, and applies one to the other. <code>λd. d</code> simply takes in an argument and simply returns it. so, what happens when you apply them ? simple, two copies of the one with d's gets created: <code>(λd.d) (λd.d)</code>. apply again, one of them eats the other, returns it back unchanged, we're left with <code>λd.d</code>. if we were doing alpha reduction, we would rename one of the <code>d</code>'s to something else, lets say </code>e</code>, so we would have <code>(λd.d) (λe.e)</code>, but our visualizer doesn't need it</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
    <h2>boolean logic</h2>
    <p>i know you're getting bored, but we're now done with the theory, so let's do some actual work !</p>
    <p>we want to represent boolean logic somehow, so we need to define booleans true and false. because there's no real way to have "data" (yet), instead of thinking about what a boolean <it>is</it>, think about what it <it>does</it>. well usually, its used to decide between two options, <code>if b: x, else y</code>, so we can represent a boolean as a function that takes in two options, and returns one or the other, depending on if its true or false. <code>TRUE</code> := <code>λt f.t</code>, and <code>FALSE</code> := <code>λt f.f</code>. note that these shorthands we're defining are really just shorthand.</p>
    <p>next we want some logic gates. a <code>NOT</code> is the easiest, it returns whatever the oposite of the input is. we can make the gate like a function that takes a boolean, and gives it false, then true. <code>NOT (long version)</code> = <code>λb.b FALSE TRUE</code> = <code>λb.b (λt f.f) (λt f.t)</code>. we can simplify this a bit, instead of returning whole new copy of the boolean, we can construct it: <code>NOT</code> := <code>\b.\t f.b f t</code>. the input <code>b</code> will return one of the other variables, and create either a true or false for us !</p>
    <p>an <code>AND</code> gate is just as simple. it takes two booleans. if the first is false, return false. otherwise return the first boolean ! <code>AND</code> := <code>λa b.a b (λt f.f)</code>. we can actually simplify this a bit; if <code>a</code> is false, we don't have to return a different false, we can just return <code>a</code> itself: <code>AND</code> := <code>λa b.a b a</code>.</p>
    <p>go play around with it a bit ! this time i'll let you type your own expressions. remember <code>\</code> is the same as <code>λ</code>.</p>
    <div class="lc-sim">
        <div class="buttons">
            <select class="examples">
                <option value="" selected disabled>pick a term to visualize</option>
                <option value="λt f.t">TRUE</option>
                <option value="λt f.f">FALSE</option>
                <option value="λb t f.b f t">NOT</option>
                <option value="(λb t f.b f t) (λt f.f)">NOT FALSE</option>
                <option value="(λa b.a b a) (λt f.t) (λt f.f)">AND TRUE FALSE</option>
                <option value="λa b.NOT (AND (NOT a) (NOT b))">OR := λa b.NOT (AND (NOT a) (NOT b))</option>
                <option value="λa b.a a b">simpler OR</option>
                <option value="(λa b.a a b) (λt f.t) (λt f.f)">OR TRUE FALSE</option>
                <option value="λa b.AND (OR a b) (NOT (AND a b))">XOR := λa b.AND (OR a b) (NOT (AND a b))</option>
                <option value="λa b.a (NOT b) b">simpler XOR</option>
            </select>
            <button class="reset">reset</button>
        </div>
        <textarea>TRUE</textarea>
        <p class="error"></p>
        <canvas width="1536" height="1024"></canvas>
    </div>
    <p>i took the liberty of defining <code>OR</code> and <code>XOR</code>, both using composition, and making them directly. obviously the direct versions are smaller, and in some way "better", but you shouldn't think about it like that, this system isn't meant to be efficient.</p>
